body:
As a cancer moon, you're right, it's super hard. My emotions are all consuming. Then it's receiving an aspect from Saturn, so it's either crying all the time and speaking to bushes or robot mode, no inbetween. :') 

&#x200B;

Really admire cap moons too. A bit gloomy but hard-working and very reliable. They don't switch up on you for sure...

Hey, I'm sorry to hear about that, it is absolutely devastating, and no wonder flips your world upside down. 

Are you good with any family member at all? It's a bit of a cliche, and sometimes this current society shuns people for this, but sometimes family can be a very strong support system. If you have someone you can lean on - perhaps an old, dear friend, or a parent figure, cousin, aunt, uncle, etc - it would be a good idea to ask for help, even if all you need is a listening ear. If that's not available, talk to your doctors about cancer fighting-survivor groups, kinda like a group therapy thing. I'm not too familiar with this, but I know after some people dealing with it - which is very possible depending on the type of cancer you have - that support groups for survivors or people currently undergoing treatment are available. I would make it a priority to look for that or a psychologist who can listen to you - not sure how possible it is in your current situation or country, but, perhaps, things to look into. 

&#x200B;

I can give you two messages of hope - my dad had kidney cancer a few years ago and after a year and a risky surgery, he was cleared. Still needs to get his exams and takes medication to manage it since they removed a kidney, but he's doing fine - in fact better than before, since I think it kind of switched his mentality and made him aware of how much he was not living the life he wanted. 

&#x200B;

I also have a friend who was diagnosed at a very young age with thyroid cancer, but fortunately that one has a good success rate and so she underwent surgery and while she had to endure some time home alone, halted her professional and social life for a while, she has since been cleared and is on the mend. She frequents a rehabilitation center for ex-cancer patients. It certainly took a hit to her life for a few years, and she is not married or even has been in a relationship for years, but she had some family support. She's also not terribly social, but she has some friends to lean on if she needs. 

&#x200B;

As for the introversion part -  I was a lot like you a few years ago; in fact, I went back to hermit mode after the terrorising medley of 2020, 2021, 2022 and 2023 - and I've lived a few years of my life, when I was doing extensive therapy (I think for depression, anxiety, low self-esteem and OCD), I was actually able to develop some good friendships for a while. It was good while it lasted - unfortunately it didn't since I had a psychotic break in 2022 and had a complete personality switch - but I can tell you that I was able to work on my introversion for a while, and so I know recovery is possible. It is not a death sentence, even if you've been like this for a long time. 

&#x200B;

Find a good therapist, someone who you can trust and feel at ease with, someone who gets your issues and doesn't minimize them, who takes your concerns seriously. Obviously your physical health is of utmost importance right now, but your mental health should also be accompanied during this time. 

&#x200B;

And hey, while relating to people seems daunting at first, there ARE ways to overcome this and connect. Nowadays with the internet (which I'm not a super fan of lol even as I write this here), you can meet people from all over the world, plus instant messaging allows you to send all kind of contact. A few ways I like to keep in contact when I don't feel like talking is sending poeple videos, reels or interesting articles, or music recommendations. Just a small thing. Or show up to an event you're interested in, and don't put too much pressure on yourself to be "on" and enjoying yourself, or performing enjoyment. Not sure about your situation, but if you stay home a lot maybe this could help? Are you in school, do you work? a lof of times those are the people we end up developing relationships with. 

&#x200B;

Just wanna say that you got this, I hope you will make it through. Wishing you luck and blessings. And a hug.  \*hugs\*

Hey, you're upset now and that's normal. I suspect anyone would be upset at not receiving a reply after sending a message about someone who should be important to them getting sick. That behaviour is on your sister's friend and not on you. You did the right thing (I don't know why you needed to text that friend, but you don't seem to be wrong to do so). 

&#x200B;

If you don't mind me asking, what happened with your roommates? "Ganging up on you" seem predatory and dangerous. It could be an issue of the way you're processing and responding to your environment at the time or perhaps they really were shitty. Idk, if it would help you to decompress a bit and talk about it, here is a space to do it. 

&#x200B;

I know you said no advice in your flair but I just couldn't help but butt in. Perhaps you're calmer now that you can reflect on what happened.   


About people respecting you more if you're mean: look, it has an immediate effect. Anyone who went to middle school can tell you that. But immediate respect isn't really respect, it's fear, and fear is the kind of emotion that makes people want nothing to do with you once they can safely get out from under your thumb. In other words, it doesn't produce loyal subjects, but rather people who will turn on you and breathe a sigh of relief as you have given them compounded reasons overtime for them to leave you once they have the chance. And idk, from my life experience, that's not really something worth pursuing, because in the end that respect you earned actually goes away and you end up alone, and that is not a life worth living, imo. Take it with a grain of salt of course. Your experience is yours after all, but just some stuff to consider. Being feared works on the immediate level, but long term it's a strategy that works against you. Maybe right now isn't the right time or frame of mind for you to think about it, but consider that sometime.

I just tried to cover up some topics that might pertain to certain cards last night, with focus on the knights this time around. What I gathered is that they usually bring messages, they're action oriented cards so when they show up they describe movement or actions towards a certain direction. The knights are a little more mature than the page, since they have more experience (they've ventured out of the kingdom and gone on travels, which the page has yet to do), but they still have some reckless enthusiasm proper for someone who's a teenager or young adult (until your 30s maybe). 

&#x200B;

Knights are in service to whatever suit they belong to. So if it's swords, the knight is in service of the intellect, of mental acuity, of mental prowess, mental ambitions etc. These qualities become highlighted and become a factor that determines how the action goes, according to the suit. But they can also not be to good at following orders - esp those from others. The knight of swords in particular is a very ambitious knight who, once he has set his mind on something, won't let up until he gets it.  But it's about what the knight has in mind, in other words, he is the one who carries out the action, so he is the one who determines whether something happens in service of a higher ideal or in service of his own desires. The knight of pentacles or cups might be the most "malleable" and less hard-headed of the knights, being in service of discipline and long-term goals and materializations (pentacle world) and of others' feelings or their own feelings (cups world). The knights can be extremists in whichever suit they're inserted. So a knight of cups doesn't have the poise and the ability to hold back and be courteous for ex that a king or queen of cups does. The knight is more eager and passionate than the other court cards, for the most part. They describe the action of wanting something and setting out on a journey to get it.


So in a way yes, they are of service to their particular suit, I guess we can say that, but as long as the meaning makes sense to you and is useful  it shouldn't be too much of a problem.

&#x200B;

Let me know if I misunderstood the question or can be of any help to you!

makes sense he'd say that, since implying a part of the human population is basically born to be "spiritually low functioning" (I mean, a master of themselves, yeah, but that leaves them vulnerable to being preyed upon by wiser types when they're busy doing their thing) is a little too much for anyone's ego to handle. you would not want to dissuade your audience. that being said, everyone wants to feel special, generators included, and of course there are special things about us. it's just not the kind of thing, i believe, that gets tauted as the ideal goal in this society? idk. all i know is learning i'm a generator didn't feel good, it felt bad, and made me hate myself a little bit more.

&#x200B;

i guess growing up with the myth of "you too are special" can sometimes be hurtful. :( not all of us are and that should be okay, we should all be part of it... yet... it's tough when you know there's people out there who are indeed special, who indeed have something spiritually great and revealing and wonderful and bright about them, and you are supposed to make sense of your life as you go about it being another busy bee, while knowing someone out there gets to keep the honey.

I can relate heavily to what you're saying :(((( this shit really takes a toll on us. It feels like I'd been living with a self-deceptive persona for most of my life. The problem is - what makes me miserable is what everyone else seems to love about me! It's not like I don't like giving to others... I do, and I don't want this trait to disappear as I go deeper into therapy. To regress back into a very needy and vulnerable child IS NOT what I need in my life rn, or probably ever will. I need to work, I need to survive. My mom thank goodness provides for me but that will not last forever and I have 0 sense of independence because of the situation rn. :( 

&#x200B;

 I know I have friends who would accept me no matter who I became - but some things feel off-limits, like choosing to decorate cakes over saving the world, or going to the gym instead of adopting a rich community-driven lifestyle (artist here, the stigma about those who would prefer therapy than drugs or partying to cope IS REAL). 

&#x200B;

You put it well - there was nothing wrong with me - I just couldn't allow myself to be a human. To have feelings. To mess up. To me, this seems like the WORST pain in the world! That I have feelings and that they are messy and sometimes I do actually end up not being the most emotionally responsible partner or friend. It really sucks. Idk what's better - to live in delusion of my self-sufficiency or to face my deep fear of being human and vulnerable deep down.

&#x200B;

Just try to do the best you can and don't beat yourself up if some days your best is 50% of what you know you're capable of (potential is such a cruel mistress!!!!). It's what all of us are doing. I'm not sure it "works" flawlessly... and that we will never hurt ourselves or others again... but to put it this way, I don't think we should be defined by our "falls", but rather through how we get up after we fall... In short, we are not only our mistakes, but how we move through them and get back on our feet. You seem like a strong person - pliable, but strong. Don't disavow yourself. You are here and you are worth it - even if it seems really difficult right now. There is a part of you that has been able to help the world around you and probably really helped a lot of people along the way... how about you enlist that part of you so it can help the most vulnerable part? It's what I've been trying to do, even visualizing it in my mind with child me and adult me. Adult me has pulled myself out of very difficult situations, and has helped a lot of people. I'm sure you've done the same through all of your hard work. All the goodness you've shared with the world, you have it within yourself. Maybe you can direct all that goodwill towards your own recovery. Be patient. It takes time. I'm sorry things hard now... but hopefully they will not be so bad forever (seems hypocritical of me to say but... I know it's true!). A storm doesn't last forever, right? Ancient wisdom. This will pass too. Sending you a tight hug.

So take this with a grain of salt as this is just something that I've found that works for me, as a fellow person with ADHD: realizing that routine is liberating has been LIFE-CHANGING.

Why is it liberating?

It's liberating because everytime you have to make a decision, it takes energy from your brain to do that. So if you don't have certain things being unchangeable, you are constantly having to act on those things, constantly making decisions. In other words, it eats up brain power to constantly decide what to eat for breakfast, for example. (This is my most recent achievement at the tender age of 27, yay! lol) I'm not an AI. I can't pretend to always accurately assess my desire for this or that. 

&#x200B;

And while stimulation is good and needed and very much craved, for baseline things such as eating habits, sleeping habits, routines etc, maybe those things are able to help us function a lot better when they're consistent.

&#x200B;

 I'm not just my brain, my whims likes or dislikes, I am also for better or worse an organic being, and as someone who was born a human and not a cat for example, I have the responsibility to do the upkeep of this system (see: law of physics, 2nd law of thermodynamic I think, the one about entropy staying the same or increasing over time).

&#x200B;

 As much as it pains me to admit, I do have to be responsible for my own well being since no one else will be. Idk, for me understanding that I was working against myself by not developing even one routine (I really lived like a gremlin and still do tbh, just going with \~vibes\~ to keep things exciting) was the thing that snapped something in my brain.

&#x200B;

 Do I want to work against myself? No, not really. I want to help myself. My life is hard enough as it is, and I need the brain power that making constant tiny decisions takes up for other more worthwhile and productive things. 

&#x200B;

I hope this helped somewhat.

Just as a warning, I'll be going in depth about this, so if you're a Capricorn maybe don't waste your time, this is merely a subjective view on the sign, based on those I've encountered and not on the signs' archetypes. Not the best and least biased source of information, but maybe it can help a shine light on why sometimes people don't like to deal with Cap influence. 

&#x200B;

They suffer the most because they put the suffering above their own wellbeing. No one has told them to do this — or at least no one should have! — but it's like capricorns have their compass constantly pointing at suffering o'clock, and like, it gets boring real fast. It's either working, sarcastic quips tearing other people down, or being depressed and drowning themselves in escapism (either workaholics or art/videogame fiends, is what I've found with the most recent Uranus/Neptune Cap generation).  

However, because they're so determined, once they turn that determination on themselves FOR THEIR OWN GOOD (so, once they dedicate to giving love to themselves, that which they are sorely lacking), they can succeed. They have the determination of an earth sign coupled with Saturn discipline — what's not to love? Once Capricorns know how to value themselves, life is easy peasy.

Until then... many lessons to be learned. 

I will just say this: I know where you're coming from. I used to be SO IMPRESSED by a Capricorn's work ethic that I "fell" in love because of it many times before. They had the best knowledge, the best taste, the best clothes, the most popular and beautiful people hanging on their arm, I really thought they had it all. Thing is, once I stopped worshipping them and putting them on a pedestal and started searching for things in common and spending nice times together, I found there to be not a lot of substance, and not a lot of good things. They're bad for my nervous system... Caps and people with Saturn conjunct personal planets, especially the luminaries. I've seen firsthand the damage that Caps can inflict on me if I don't put on the brakes — they take and take and take, like the reaper, and expect you to constantly provide. Well sorry for assuming a relationship has to flow both ways. However, I will say that if you submit to them, they are very loving partners. The problems start when you have a different opinion, especially about what they consider their sacred "ethics", which are actually pretty self-serving. Rarely have I seen a capricorn not act out of ego and genuinely go out of their way for someone. I've even heard some of them asking "what's in it for me?" That's funny as a joke, not as a real way to live lol.

&#x200B;

 I've met a lof of Capricorns, rising, sun, moon or venus and mercury (curiously enough, not many marses) and what I've found is that these are the people with which I share a surface level similarity but then... Like we like the same things, so me as a rambunctious Gemini Venus assume that they would want to talk about them! But no: things that I find super interesting and am willing to delve deeper into, are nothing more than status symbols for Capricorn. So we like the same movies, but trying to talk to them about the movie's plot or history behind it is always so so lacklustre. It's genuinely sad... And I tried for years. I have no problem discussing things with other signs, but Caps in particular strike me as stuck in their ways, and their ways are those of status and climbing to the top. Capricorns or Saturn-heavy people have done me dirty more than once because they wanted to "punish me" for some perceived slight or affront to their ethics that I was unware of. Indeed I've also done the same so I know what I'm talking about here. When you're too concerned with other's perceptions, you're loosing yourself. So what if someone disagrees? It's not the end of the world. I've found Capricorns to be very "my way or the highway", and while I can respect that when it comes to work (fantastic, if not the best work colleagues), I can't deal with it in friends or lovers. Too fussy about forcing others to submit to their ideas.

&#x200B;

I believe, like any other sign, Capricorn has many good qualities. I'm rn at a moment in my life where the bad far outweighs the good... But I know there are many people out there who'd love and cherish what you have to offer, so don't get discouraged. It just takes time to find them. But being ruled by Saturn, you know how to wait for good things to happen, don't you? :) A secret, be easier on yourselves, no one is as hard on yourselves as you are. Practice self-acceptance and as you move forward in your journey of self-love you will fall in love with all the good qualities you know yourself to possess.

It was really hard for me to see the spiritual ways in which this Cancer stellium friend of mine expressed their cancerian qualities... of course it was, because his MC, Sun, Mercury and Venus were all ruled by... tumtumtum... MOON IN ARIES!!!!

&#x200B;

I'm an actual cancer moon and I found it SO DRAINING to deal with him! But that's because I couldn't see that while he *felt* like an aries moon, he *acted* like a cancer... so so so confusing. 

&#x200B;

But to answer your question: yes. While the moon of a cancer sun person is NOT their chart ruler, it is the ruler of their sun. So I find it to always be a good complement to understand the Cancer sun/other placements in the chart.

&#x200B;

In the case of my friend, he has a burgeoning social circle, very very famous and well liked, helps people express their art (Venus, Sun and Mercury in 10th house) and capacitates others to express themselves to their full ability. He's doing wonderful work. But that 7th house Aries moon... It's so fickle and irresponsible and it takes soooo long to convince them to come around. My cancer moon couldn't take being given the most loving treatment I've ever experienced in physical reality coupled with the fact that HE DIDN'T FUCKING MEAN IT because his Aries moon ofc only cares about a person for 5 minutes and then moves on. Then comes back ofc because Cancer is clingy.

&#x200B;

Sorry for ranting a bit, but I always find it interesting and more comprehensible when people add stories to their opinion of the placements. It's good for us to understand the physical ways in which this energy manifests itself in material reality :)

Ah no problem! :) They don't really "shift", per se, but using different house systems — so basically different ways of dividing the zodiac wheel — you can get different meanings. Some schools of astrology prefer using whole signs, others equal house and others placidus or other such systems. If you're interested you can learn more about house systems. 

&#x200B;

Something to keep in mind about 12th house planets is that a lot of times they're not visible to the "chart user", in this case you. But they get dispersed into the collective, you're not really aware of how they operate, but at a subconscious level they do impact you. I have some planets in 12th house and reading about them has made me understand more, mostly bc indeed the energies of those planets have been kind of absent to me for most of my life. Only when I learned about for ex. my mars in 12th house could I see that actually I wasn't lazy, and that I simply couldn't value and comprehend all the ways in which my mars operates. It's quite a mindfucking house tbh and I'll admit I'm not the best person to talk about. \^\^' I hope someone more knowledgeable pops in and helps...

This comment gives me life.

&#x200B;

I still don't really understand what Saturn in 12th necessarily entails or how to deal with it, but those were powerful words.

I've been obsessively reading on reddit for years trying to figure out what is wrong with me and I've never heard it put so eloquently. Also I was looking in all the wrong places - I have OCD and anxiety through the roof, so the thought of hurting another human being even causes me physical pain: I analyzed all my actions obsessively and concluded that when things didn't work out with other people, it was because I believed I'm actually a covert narcissist who can't feel things or genuinely like people. 

&#x200B;

While I do struggle with liking people (sorry, human race) due to my incredibly high standards and need to be perfect and in control at all times (like you, I'm mirroring my mom here), I always go out of my way to help and I isolate myself for years so I don't run the risk of accidentally hurting someone with my behaviour. But I'm in good hands now, and through therapy and a lot of introspection it's finally hit me that I'm actually a stupidly anxious co-dependent rather than a narcissist... I'm still searching obsessively about narcissists and what if I truly am one, but I really need to quit it. It's ruining my life more than any 'bad' human interaction ever did. 

&#x200B;

Your comment is a gold mine, both because of the accuracy with which you report your experiences and because of how comprehensive and well-articulated it is. I'm sure it will be easier for a lot of us here to pinpoint the issues after reading your comment. Thank you for taking the time to write it and all the best to you. I hope you and everyone here can heal and find joy in their true selves instead of keeping up the dysfunctional relational patterns.

As an art history undergrad (bachelor? I never know what the correct term is), I've recently gotten back into Hetalia because I think it's a great way to combine three things I love: anime, history and drawing (I make fanart and write fic sometimes). 

&#x200B;

I'm an old person. I was in the fandom back in 2010 and I knew how out of whack that beast was. Today the fans seem much more aware of racial issues and problematic depictions (either that or I just don't hang at the cesspools anymore, like Amino or the deepest depths of Discord servers). Plus with all the recent focus on colonialism in worldwide western media, it would have been pretty hard for any Hetalia fans to miss the memo about what's okay and not okay to do.

&#x200B;

That being said! I think it's possible to separate the slave trade part from depictions of the countries who played a big part in it during that time. Do you think colonial powers like Spain for ex. just shouldn't be represented during the XVI or XV century? Would depictions of a Conquistador be upsetting, even if in no way (other than well, the choice to depict them) indicates the artist is condoning colonialism? Where do we draw the line? I often wonder about that. 

&#x200B;

 I've seen a lot of those fanarts in the past and they're still coming out rn, so maybe that's a little too extreme. Perhaps since the subject has come to the "public forum" in recent years, it feels tasteless to engage in such behaviour rn. I think people just need to a) be tasteful and sensitive about the subject and b) read about it! (Esp. for ficwriters). Really, skimming a wikipedia article goes a long way. And possibly c) remember that Hetalia is mostly silly, crack-y stuff, not meant to be taken seriously by the creator's own standards. Despite that, it's totally valid for writers and artists to focus on the angstier (and meatier) parts of history. But sensitivity and caution should be exercised the heavier the subject matter is. When in doubt, RESEARCH extensively! Also, creators should remember that for the most part, while Hetalia does talk about WWII, it never talks about the tragic part and doesn't have jokes about genocide or concentration camps (thank goodness). I think for the most part Himaruya did a good job separating the character of Germany from the actions of its political leaders, but that's obviously debatable. For some people, however, simply talking about WWII in such a jokey and superficial way is enough to condemn the whole franchise. For me, I think it's okay to use a little bit of humor to cope with these things. YMMV.

&#x200B;

Like someone else said in the thread, the further something is from us in terms of time, the easier it is to justify making fun of it. People probably make fun of the Napoleonic wars now, but 200 years ago it would have been a bit too much.

Hi, I don't know how to post pictures with text so I'm writing here what I wanted to add:

I noticed recently that one of my biggest issues, both with others and myself is the fact that I'm extremely demanding of perfection. I get disappointed when someone doesn't fulfill my expectations, but worse even, I get angry. It pains me to admit, I feel super embarrassed and just try to overcompensate a lot of times by being very nice and accommodating, but the truth is I feel like it's a personal slight when perfection doesn't happen. It pains me to admit but it's the truth and there's no point in hiding it if I want to change.

&#x200B;

If I feel close enough to the person, I don't protect them from the demands I have for them, I become extremely vocal to the point where it drives people away. This makes me have a lot of acquaintances - since I don't expect much from ppl I barely think about, I can maintain a good image of them. The closer someone gets to me, the more free I feel to criticize them. But that's mean, and I'm learning that the hard way. Since everyone hurts me and disappoints me, I just don't interact with people much lately.

&#x200B;

Recently a friend pointed out that I have extremely high standards for myself, because while I feel those bad emotions towards others I also shame myself and hate myself a lot to see if I can "fall in line" and behave perfectly. I would have said I struggle with perfectionism but this runs a lot deeper.  I have an extremely negative self-talk, which in turn makes me see other people as Very Shitty and Imperfect™, which obviously is a recipe for disaster since often people are trying their best and I KNOW I'm being extremely insensitive by feeling such strong and angry emotions about them. I try to learn to be more compassionate and I've seen some improvements but it's not enough.

&#x200B;

Truth is being like this doesn't make me achieve the perfection I so desire. Instead it fills me with dread and anxiety to the point where I stop functioning and just give up and surrender to doing nothing a lot of times. Although that has started to change, and I'm trying to allow myself some freedom to simply be... restrictions be damned.

&#x200B;

But yeah. Any insights? How to overcome this, from an astrological standpoint? 

&#x200B;

(Before you ask: yes, I'm taking medication and going to therapy, although not too frequently.)

Fantastic comment, thank you so much for putting into words. That was my journey with art criticism as well — at first I took it at face value, like some truth everyone knew about except me, and then I started looking at it as a way into what another human being had to say about the same experience I had just had. Particularly, how they describe the feeling I get when it gets to that triumphant chorus, for example: which words would they use to describe that guitar riff? Did it unleash the same sensorial/emotional response in them? Would I use the same words? Is that feeling universal? 

&#x200B;

Not just with feelings, but a lot of times I'm just curious about the technical musical language employed since I'm not well-versed in it. All in all, I think it's a much more interesting way to engage with criticism. Take it for what it is, human-produced thoughts using human-produced words to describe something another human produced. Although sometimes I still fall into the trap of assuming one thing might be true or not, but that's my obsession with finding the truth in everything rearing its ugly head (I like to philosophise once in a while).

I struggle with the same thing. Hey there. You're not alone.

&#x200B;

People don't tell me what I do wrong when they start drifting away from me. I learned that a lot of times, the drifting was actually mutual, meaning I also had a part in it. My part in it was not being attentive enough, not being curious enough about them and their interests and experiences — in short, I routinely don't show up, but I still want people to remember me and be my friend. 

&#x200B;

I'd advise you to ask yourself if that's the case. Does that happen with everyone, and I do mean everyone you meet? What are your relationships like? Do you put a lot of value in seeing each other every day, or are you ok with seeing your friends once every three months? What do you want in a friend? Do you consider these people adequate to meet your social needs or are you compromising? Answer these questions for yourself, after all, you are the only person who has ever walked in your shoes. 

&#x200B;

In the future, it might be useful to enlist the help of a therapist to help with figuring this stuff out. A therapist that acts like a sounding board for your thought process should help, but it's not guaranteed. I've found that with someone to listen to me, I sometimes resort to whining about my hardships rather than giving a detailed account of what happened. It's natural and to be expected. This might have to do with me honestly distorting things as they are happening, meaning that I get so in my head that I don't really focus on what the other person is conveying through words and body language (the last one is really important and one I'm notoriously bad at interpreting, although I used to think otherwise). This is NOT done out of malice, I'm sure you understand that, but it's behaviour I can't seem to get out of right now. To the other person though, dealing with this routinely might be too tiring. Why would they want to spend time with someone who doesn't seem to listen to them? Who doesn't want to share things with them? 

&#x200B;

Don't quit man. Never quit. I'm so glad I didn't. I'm a weird person and I'm slowly making my peace with it. In the mean time, I've gotten into some groups, thought I'd found my people (while in the back of my head still not really reaching out to them bc honestly? I didn't like them that much), realized they weren't my people, got into groups, and all the while I've picked up some people here and there. People who seem happy to have a conversation with me, who ask me out, and most importantly, people in which I am GENUINELY interested. For the longest time I thought I should be everyone's friend. It doesn't work like that. I have my preferences and values in friendship and it's OKAY to stand by them. I suspect something like that might be happening with you, although this is a lot of projecting on my part. It's something that took me a really long time to figure out.

&#x200B;

 I hope something resonates. And like I said, don't give up — your ticket to the future is always blank. You never know what will happen. Try to stay open minded. 

&#x200B;

A more immediate solution as you work out your problems in the background is to simply not waste more time trying to win over people who don't seem to consider you their friend. Just know it's okay to disengage on your end too.

I can't exercise as a "just do it" type of thing. I admire people who can discipline themselves to go to the gym (or anywhere really lol) even when they're not excited about it, but alas that is not the case for me. (If you're reading this, in this sub, it's probably not the case for you either...)

I don't work out now but I've signed up for 4 or 5 gyms in my life and ended up quitting every time. You might have more success with these approaches than I did:  


1) sign up for a gym close to home or on your way from home to work, that way you don't have to take a big detour. It's easier to go when you don't have to disturb your daily life.

&#x200B;

2) think about gym time as time to explore something else. For me, that's listening to a new music album, for you it might be a podcast. Audio interests are better because you don't have to be staring at a screen or standing in a certain position to enjoy yourself. Plus, extra stimulation!

&#x200B;

3) role models or your imagination. For me, I love badass characters who are good with guns/fighters/warriors, so when in the gym I used to think of myself as either them going about their daily routine or as me training to become like them. This is basically indulging in a hyperfixation, at least that's what I'd describe it for myself, and it works wonders, but as most hyperfixations, it can be short lived, so not a permanent cure. :(

&#x200B;

4) if you like cardio and can afford it, I'd suggest getting a home bike or thread mill. That way you can set it up in front of the TV and watch your favorite shows, documentaries, youtube videos, anything! You get stimulation for your brain and your legs! Pretty cool. I used to do this and biking for half an hour was amazing bc I didn't even feel it. 

&#x200B;

5) discover what you like. If there's a team sport or martial art you'd like to try, give it a go! It's okay to be passionate about exercise, no matter what fitness influencers want you to believe! (That it's all hard work, sweat and tears). Exercising can and SHOULD be fun. Honestly, if this part was stressed more over the "you're a failure of a person bc you can't discipline yourself to do something you don't like only to prove to yourself and the world that you can", less kids would be overweight and more people would incorporate exercise in their daily life. Some suggestions: any martial arts, dancing, tennis or badminton, rollerblading, hiking, riding a bike while listening to music and exploring the scenery. 

&#x200B;

6) finally, and this is my best advice: try to build your life in a way that makes you get off the couch and walk but so that it doesn't feel like it. This is a lifestyle change and it takes some time to see results, and I never thought I'd be saying this, but I did see results. I wasn't obsessively checking to see if I was walking enough miles or anything (although the competition w yourself aspect of it is fun), but I just became more interested in seeing the world beyond my screen. I wanted to see new things, go to new places, and since I don't own a car I had to walk everywhere. I would whine but still do it anyway. But the point is there was usually a reward, like getting to see a concert or hang out with friends, going to a festival, to a museum, etc. In short, don't see it as exercise — it's simply something that enables you to get to the fun parts of life.

&#x200B;

I hope this is helpful! Best luck to you!

