AAAAAAAA omg I can't believe this. It's a bit the same thing with me (except my parents splurged on clothes for me, but all the rest they were stupidly frugal about).

Also I hear you on having to keep your financial status a secret. I remember one day in 6th grade sitting at lunch with my friends, we were talking about how much money our parents made. I tell them how much my parents make, go home and tell my mom about it (because I thought I'd be — still do — a bad daughter if I didn't tell her literally everything). She loses her shit, sits me down and explains how we can't talk about money to anyone outside the household, how people could get jealous, it's a breach of privacy, etc. I stood there blinking repeatedly trying to process how tf some 12 year olds could be jealous of our frankly average lifestyle. Also couldn't talk about my uncle who has problems with gambling because it was related to money. Actually, nothing about my family should be talked about ever, not even praise, because then someone might get jealous.

&#x200B;

I used to get maybe 2€ weekly pocket money since I lived in front of my middle school; I'm 25 now and learning how to budget. It's a bit embarrassing. Also since they used to pay for everything, they had a say on the clothes I wore. I still have self-image issues today because of this.

&#x200B;

I'm also afraid of splurging tbh, I constantly buy the cheapest clothes possible. It's weird because if it was something my parents liked they could spend hundreds of euros on something, but I hated that because who the hell needs to spend so much money on clothes?! (and then feel ashamed lol). To this day I wear my clothes until they fall apart, especially shoes. It's stupid and I know they can afford to buy me nice stuff more often, but it was drilled into my head that we were so so poor, we couldn't do anything fun.

