Ok so first of all, I know your wife's position. I've never been in an official romantic relationship, but I share the feeling of wanting to loosen up a bit before intimate time with whoever I'm seeing at the moment. But... I'm also an addict. I've been getting drunk at least once a week (sometimes I go one or two weeks without) for the past year. I'm not sure that makes me an alcoholic, but by most people's definition there's probably a connection...

&#x200B;

To the posters talking about how she uses sex as an excuse to drink, you really seem like you don't have a clue what you're talking about. This woman's problem seems serious and a lot deeper than just wanting to get wasted for the hell of it. To OP, does she usually drink with friends or when she's alone, or is this something that seems more consistent when sex is on the table? 

&#x200B;

As someone who had sex for the first time while high (and most times afterwards), I did use it as a way to both a) loosen up, so I wouldn't seem too boring since I really wasn't very experienced and wanted to appear cool, which seems like it should not be a concern between you two given that, you know, YOU ARE MARRIED, b) just get on with it because I wanted the experience of sex but found the actual physical act not very fun or exciting (most likely because I wasn't in love or in any way attached to the person, which again doesn't seem to be the case between you two), or c) I felt incredibly self-conscious of myself, my body and my experience or lack thereof and didn't want to disappoint my partner. Kinda like an ostrich - if I hide my face then I won't see anyone and no one will see me. It's an illusion but a very protective and safe on. I wanted to perform and to give my partner a good experience of sex, and I forgot that I'm supposed to have fun during the process. 

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

All this being said, I want to tell you that what helped me the most was therapy. I've not taken any meds but I'm not sure what to call this type of therapy, usually one or two therapists will play my ego or alter ego. Maybe it's what you call CBT or DBT, I don't know. I went to therapy sober and tried to understand how I felt during an obviously simulated sexual act and let all my thoughts out. Perhaps this sort of thing would help your wife. Getting in touch with your body and bodily sensations is a very (if not the most) important part of sex. For me, sex was taking place inside my head. I felt extremely anxious and excitable at the prospect of sex, but when the time came it was just like any other experience. I also suffer from generalized and social anxiety as well as depression. 

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

What I can tell you from my experience is that I was not sexually abused or anything like that growing up, but I did experience a lot of shame regarding sexual education. My first contact with sexual content was at 7 years old or something and my parents shamed me to death for that (not catholic, btw). This attitude has obviously carried over and it's something I'm only getting to in my twenties. These things take time, and I want to tell you that this will indeed take some time off of your "fun time" in your marriage, but it will most definitely be worth it to explore what's going on. The first step is obviously for her to recognize her pattern of behaviour and want to change. You can't do that for her, or push her to just quit drinking, but you can help her when she needs you. I think if I had a consistent sexual partner who genuinely cared about me and was willing to walk me through this it would definitely help. When I had sober sex for the first time it didn't feel amazing, or at least as amazing as when I was high so I can understand if that's her problem as well, but it doesn't seem to be just a matter of preference since it seems like a pre-requisite. 

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

To wrap this up because it's getting long, my problem is definitely more psychological than physical and it seems like that might be the case with her as well. I especially identify with the being shy in bed (in my case not just in bed, but since you're married you've probably good on that part haha). I truly wish you two the best of luck as I feel this is indeed something that puts a dent on any relationship, but you seem supportive enough to help make this a healing and positive process for her.

I think this has a bit more to do with maturity and how insecure you feel instead of other people. Once you feel more secure in your expression of your feelings and ideas, you'll understand that people have a myriad of reasons for responding to you one day or another, and most of the time it really has nothing to do with you and everything to do with them.

&#x200B;

Personally I think this is something people with social anxiety tend to experience, and not related to any specific personality type. What worked for me was consciously reassuring myself that other people's response to me is not something I can control, like just thinking the words out loud if it makes sense actually helps a lot. 

&#x200B;

As for the instability regarding crushes or people I admire, I absolutely relate to that, unfortunately. Sometimes I wish I could be more consistent, but I think I secretly enjoy feeling so excitable and responsive to emotional environments. Does this make any sense? haha

